const content = "\u003cp>A serious Filipino-Western relationship asks her to consider your actual life, not a warmer version of it. She needs to know where you live, what you want, who already depends on you, and whether you can travel on a real calendar. Warm sentences that hide those facts do not make you look open. They make the next months expensive for both of you.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>We offer this as educational dating guidance for men who want that kind of relationship, not as a script for first messages and not as a promise about how any later meeting will go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Write for the woman who might one day have to plan around your life, not for a stranger scrolling for atmosphere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"write-for-a-meeting-you-could-keep\">Write For A Meeting You Could Keep\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Distance makes it cheap to perform. A list of hobbies, travel photos, and compliments does not answer the questions that appear as soon as two adults consider a first visit. She still needs to know the city you sleep in, the relationship you are offering, the people already in your household, and the time you can give. Those points are not unromantic. They are how a serious conversation avoids a surprise that should have been on the page.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Write as if she might sit across from you. That does not mean publishing your biography. It means every claim should still be true when she can see your face and your week. If you would need to walk a sentence back later, it does not belong in the profile.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An optional draft, only if it matches you, might read: \"I am 44, live in Houston, divorced, two children with me during the school year. I want a long-term relationship that could lead to marriage. I can take two weeks away next year. I cannot relocate in the first twelve months.\" That is a person with limits. If it is not your life, do not copy it. If it is close, keep the shape: facts, intent, constraint.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A thinner profile that you can defend is more useful than a decorated one you cannot. Attention from people who wanted a fantasy is not a gain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"use-photos-that-still-look-like-you\">Use Photos That Still Look Like You\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Recent photos are the first check against the man who will later appear on a call or at an airport. Use pictures from the last few months, in ordinary light, of your current face, hair, and body. Include one clear head-and-shoulders shot and one full-length shot. A group photo only helps if it is obvious which person you are.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A ten-year-old gym picture, a cropped wedding shot, or a face hidden by sunglasses does not fail because it is unflattering. It fails because it asks her to trust a face she has not seen. Take new photos if that is all you have. A kitchen, a street, or a weekend walk is enough. Avoid heavy filters and pictures of cars, hotel lobbies, or other people's children. The photo should answer whether this is you now, not whether this is the life you want to imply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Recent photos still do not prove identity. A current picture of the right man can sit next to a false job, a false city, or a false promise to visit. Photos cut one kind of surprise. They do not certify character, income, or intent. A later video conversation or a family introduction, if you both choose those steps, still does not prove that everything on the profile is true. What the photos can do is stop you from starting with a story you already know is false.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"name-the-place-you-actually-live\">Name The Place You Actually Live\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Location is the geography of any later meeting, not a brand. If you live in Manchester, write Manchester. If you spend a few months a year somewhere else, say that as travel, not as a second hometown. A fashionable city might attract more replies. It also starts the relationship with a lie she will have to discover, and discovery at that point is not a cute misunderstanding. It is a reason to stop.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>City and country are usually enough for a first reading. Street names, your employer's address, and the cafe you visit every morning are not. Keep your home address and precise daily routine out of conversations with someone you do not know. Hold the same line on the public page. She needs the region she would be talking across. She does not need a map to your door.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If work moves you between cities, name the place you actually sleep most nights, then mention travel as a separate fact. \"Based in Perth, in the Philippines for work twice a year\" is usable. \"Worldwide\" is not. Accuracy here is practical: it lets her decide whether the distance is something she wants to consider at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"state-relationship-intent-in-plain-language\">State Relationship Intent In Plain Language\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Relationship intent is the difference between a serious profile and a polite stall. If you want marriage, say you want marriage. If you want a long-term relationship and you are not ready to name marriage, say that. If you only want casual chat, do not present yourself as a man building a household with someone in the Philippines.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Clarity is not a speech about destiny. It is a few sentences she can test against your time. \"I want a partner I can meet in person within a year and build a shared household with\" is a claim about pace. \"I enjoy talking to women from the Philippines\" is not. The first can be discussed. The second can consume months without ever becoming a plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Do not outsource intent to nationality. Wanting a Filipino-Western relationship does not tell her whether you will respect her faith, her family decisions, or her wish to stay in her city. Those are individual questions, and she is the person who can answer them. For how respect shows up once you are talking, read our guide on \u003ca href=\"https://filipinawest.com/guides/dating-a-filipina-respectfully\">dating respectfully across cultures\u003c/a>. On the profile, name your own goal without casting her as a type you shopped for.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If you already know you would only ever ask her to move, write that. If you would consider living in the Philippines later, write it as a possibility you have not priced, not as a vow. Ambiguity on this point is not kindness. It is unpaid labour she does while other serious options close.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"treat-travel-as-time-you-can-actually-give\">Treat Travel As Time You Can Actually Give\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Travel availability is where many profiles become fiction. Writing that you can visit \"soon\" without checking time off, savings, who cares for your children, or whether your passport is valid is not optimism. It is a promise you have not costed. A serious counterpart will eventually need a window, not a mood.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>State what you can do in the next year, as far as you know it today. That might be \"I can visit for two weeks after June\" or \"I cannot travel until this contract ends in November.\" If you cannot relocate, say so. If a first visit would depend on someone else taking the children, say that the visit has to fit that care, not that love will arrange it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A first visit is a plan two adults make when they already know each other well enough to want it. The profile should not pretend the ticket is booked. It should show whether a visit is even possible. Call schedule works the same way. If you work nights in the United States and she works days in Cebu, the hours you can actually talk belong in your thinking. You do not need a timetable on the page. You do need to stop implying that you are free every evening if you are not.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Money for travel is part of the same honesty. You do not need to publish your salary. You do need to avoid implying unlimited mobility when your budget, leave, or dependents say otherwise. The constraint is the useful fact. The performance of being easy to meet is not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"share-commitments-without-handing-over-your-family\">Share Commitments Without Handing Over Your Family\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Children, former marriages, and other people who depend on you belong on a serious profile because they shape time, money, and any later household. Hiding them does not protect privacy. It delays the conversation that decides whether the relationship is possible. The person who needed those facts will still need them. You only lose the chance to be the one who said them first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The line to hold is specific versus identifying. You can write that you have children, a general age range if you wish, and whether they live with you. Do not publish your children’s names or school details in your dating profile. The same rule covers photographs of minors, school uniforms, and the street outside their home. FilipinoCupid publishes \u003ca href=\"https://www.filipinocupid.com/en/general/datingsafety\">profile privacy guidance\u003c/a> that treats those details as information a stranger should not be able to take from a dating page.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Prior commitments include more than children: a parent you care for, a job that cannot move, a legal process already underway, a faith community that will matter if you marry. Name the commitment, not the paperwork. \"I look after my father three days a week\" is enough. His clinic, his full name, and your street are not.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Privacy is not the opposite of honesty. It is the split between facts a partner needs and data a stranger can use. For the wider picture of \u003ca href=\"https://filipinawest.com/safety\">dating safety\u003c/a> as conversations continue, practise that split on the profile first: enough truth to be known, not enough detail to be found. You can be clear about being a father without making your children searchable. You can be clear about where you live without publishing the route you walk at 7 a.m.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"describe-values-not-a-role-you-invented-for-her\">Describe Values, Not A Role You Invented For Her\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Filipino-Western relationship is two people. Profiles that list what she is supposed to be like, or what a Western man \"brings,\" turn her into a function. She cannot give mutual consent to a part you wrote before you met her.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Write your own values in the first person. If faith matters to you, say which practice you keep and that you will respect hers without assuming what it is. If you want a partner who wants children, say that. If you do not want more children, say that too. If you are prepared to meet her relatives when she chooses that, say you are prepared. Do not announce that her family will require it. Ask her.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avoid shopping-list compliments about appearance, temperament, or rescue. They tell her you are hiring a character. An optional line that stays on the right side of this, only if it is true: \"I am Catholic, I want marriage, and I will not mock anyone's faith. I hope to build a household where both of our families are welcome, at a pace we both agree.\" If that is not you, write the true version. Personal boundaries belong here as limits you will keep: what you will not rush, what you will not fund for a stranger, what you will not ask her to hide.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Idealised ethnic expectations fail for a simple reason. They are about a group. She is one adult with her own consent, her own pace, and her own conditions for a first visit. Your profile should make room for that person, not for a story you brought with you.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-draft-as-if-you-were-deciding-whether-to-reply\">Read The Draft As If You Were Deciding Whether To Reply\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before anyone else sees the page, read it as the woman who has to choose. Check three things. Would each photo still match you this month. Would each fact still be true if she asked for it again on a call. Have you given away anything that belongs only to your children, your address, or your daily route.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Then read it for tone. Serious does not mean grim. It means you are not performing a holiday version of yourself. Cut jokes that hide the point. Cut paragraphs about income that try to stand in for character. Cut any sentence that exists only because you think men on these sites are supposed to sound a certain way.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If a friend who already knows you would raise an eyebrow at a claim, fix the claim. It is cheaper to correct a line now than to explain it for months. That is the whole trade-off: a truer page will be ignored by people who wanted a fantasy, and a decorated page will attract attention you cannot keep. For a relationship that has to survive distance, the first outcome is the one that saves time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If what you have written is honest about photos, location, intent, travel, children, and privacy, you already have the profile. You can \u003ca href=\"https://filipinawest.com/signup\">create a FilWest account\u003c/a> when you want a place to put that work. The form will not invent the honesty. That part is yours.\u003c/p>";
